 @rattlehead999  Zen 2 is not overpriced. Just because AMD was able to make a higher profit margin this time around doesn't mean it's automatically "overpriced". If you want overpriced, Intel's 9900K and 9700K are perfect for that; or better yet, their entire lineup in general. The fact that the 3700X was the same launch price as the 2700X, yet being around 25% faster was pretty good. Or you could've had Intel's 5% performance improvement, if even that, per year if you want to know what price gouging really is. The 2600K sold for $317 at launch, and the 3770K the next year was priced at $332. And AMD was even nice enough to drop prices on Zen and Zen+ processors. As for Intel? A used 6700K or 7700K is still sold for ridiculous prices.


AMD operated on such a low profit margin back then because they had no choice. No one was going to buy an FX-8350 for over $150 because of how pathetic it's performance was even compared to an i5. Even the highest clocked FX CPUs were still annihilated by locked Core i7s of the time, so AMD could not afford to price them higher in risk of losing even more sales. AMD sold their RX 480 for $229 maximum because of their mindshare and performance. And even when the RX 470 was the same price and faster than a GTX 1050 Ti, the 1050 Ti still outsold the RX 470 simply because of mindshare. When people saw that NVIDIA was in the lead massively, they'd rather pick the "winning company" rather than buy something from the "losing company".


I'll agree that the 5500 XT's price is too high. The 4GB card should be priced at the 1650 Super's level, with the 8GB being $20 more.

